

1. Core data model

States

Nano state

Label: NanoState

Symbol: (x(t))

Content: high‑dimensional fields (positions, charges, temperatures, etc.)

Technology state

Label: TechState

Symbol: (z(t))

Content: device‑level metrics (currents, gains, efficiencies, modes, etc.)

Compression state

Label: CompState

Symbol: (y(t))

Content: compressed fields / reduced coordinates

Grand state

Label: GrandState

Symbol: (w(t) = (z(t), y(t)))

Parameters

Physical: Theta → (\theta)

Design: Lambda → (\lambda)

Compression: Beta → (\beta)

2. Equation modules as code units

Think of each equation as a module with a clean interface.

2.1 Fused nanofield core

Equation

[ \mathcal{N}(x, \dot{x}, u, t; \theta) = 0 ]

Interface (conceptual)

interface NanofieldCore {
  evalResidual(x: NanoState, xDot: NanoState, u: Control, t: number, theta: Theta): Residual;
}

You then wrap this with an integrator:

interface NanofieldIntegrator {
  step(x: NanoState, u: Control, t: number, dt: number, theta: Theta): NanoState;
}

2.2 Meta‑technology module

Projection

[ z = \Psi(x, u; \theta, \lambda) ]

Equation

[ \mathcal{M}_{\text{tech}}(z, \dot{z}, t; \theta, \lambda) = 0 ]

Interface

interface TechProjection {
  project(x: NanoState, u: Control, theta: Theta, lambda: Lambda): TechState;
}

interface MetaTechnology {
  evalResidual(z: TechState, zDot: TechState, t: number, theta: Theta, lambda: Lambda): Residual;
  step(z: TechState, t: number, dt: number, theta: Theta, lambda: Lambda): TechState;
}

2.3 Meta‑compression module

Projection

[ y = \Phi(x; \theta, \beta) ]

Equation

[ \mathcal{M}_{\text{comp}}(y, \dot{y}, t; \theta, \beta) = 0 ]

Interface

interface CompressionProjection {
  project(x: NanoState, theta: Theta, beta: Beta): CompState;
}

interface MetaCompression {
  evalResidual(y: CompState, yDot: CompState, t: number, theta: Theta, beta: Beta): Residual;
  step(y: CompState, t: number, dt: number, theta: Theta, beta: Beta): CompState;
}

2.4 Grand meta‑equation orchestrator

Equation

[ \mathcal{G}(w, \dot{w}, t; \theta, \lambda, \beta) = 0,\quad w = (z, y) ]

Interface

interface GrandMetaEquation {
  step(
    x: NanoState,
    z: TechState,
    y: CompState,
    u: Control,
    t: number,
    dt: number,
    theta: Theta,
    lambda: Lambda,
    beta: Beta
  ): { x: NanoState; z: TechState; y: CompState };
}

Implementation of step:

Advance x via NanofieldIntegrator.

Recompute z = Ψ(x, u; θ, λ).

Recompute y = Φ(x; θ, β).

Optionally refine z and y via their own evolution equations (MetaTechnology.step, MetaCompression.step) if they have dynamics beyond pure projection.

3. Engine runtime loop

Conceptual main loop:

function runSimulation(state: {
  x: NanoState;
  z: TechState;
  y: CompState;
  t: number;
}, params: { theta: Theta; lambda: Lambda; beta: Beta }) {

  while (state.t < tFinal) {
    const u = controlLaw(state.t, state.x, state.z, state.y);

    const next = grandMetaEquation.step(
      state.x,
      state.z,
      state.y,
      u,
      state.t,
      dt,
      params.theta,
      params.lambda,
      params.beta
    );

    state.x = next.x;
    state.z = next.z;
    state.y = next.y;
    state.t += dt;

    logger.log(state);
  }
}

That loop is the operational form of your grand meta‑equation.

